WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.870 --> 00:00:05.385
Hi and welcome back.

2
00:00:05.385 --> 00:00:08.400
You've learned about the
ResNet architecture,

3
00:00:08.400 --> 00:00:10.470
you've learned about
Inception net.

4
00:00:10.470 --> 00:00:13.260
In this video, you'll
learn about MobileNets,

5
00:00:13.260 --> 00:00:15.510
which is another foundational

6
00:00:15.510 --> 00:00:18.029
convolutional neural
network architecture

7
00:00:18.029 --> 00:00:19.695
used for computer vision.

8
00:00:19.695 --> 00:00:23.190
Using MobileNets will allow
you to build and deploy

9
00:00:23.190 --> 00:00:26.985
new networks that work even
in low compute environment,

10
00:00:26.985 --> 00:00:29.970
such as a mobile
phone. Let's dive in.

11
00:00:29.970 --> 00:00:33.630
Why do you need another
neural network architecture?

12
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:35.040
It turns out in other

13
00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:36.320
neural networks
you've learned about

14
00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:39.440
so far are quite
computationally expensive.

15
00:00:39.440 --> 00:00:43.610
If you want your neural network
to run on a device with

16
00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:47.645
less powerful CPU or
a GPU at deployment,

17
00:00:47.645 --> 00:00:50.390
then there's another neural
network architecture called

18
00:00:50.390 --> 00:00:53.560
the MobileNet that could
perform much better.

19
00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:56.810
I hope to share a view
into this video how

20
00:00:56.810 --> 00:01:00.905
the depthwise separable
convolution works.

21
00:01:00.905 --> 00:01:03.950
Let's first revisit what the
normal convolution does,

22
00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:05.480
and then we'll modify it to build

23
00:01:05.480 --> 00:01:08.455
the depthwise
separable convolution.

24
00:01:08.455 --> 00:01:10.700
In the normal convolution,

25
00:01:10.700 --> 00:01:18.615
you may have an input image
that is some n by n by n_c,

26
00:01:18.615 --> 00:01:21.170
where n_c is the
number of channels.

27
00:01:21.170 --> 00:01:24.680
Six by six by three
channels in this case,

28
00:01:24.680 --> 00:01:28.030
and you want to convolve
it with a filter that

29
00:01:28.030 --> 00:01:32.705
is f by f by n_c,

30
00:01:32.705 --> 00:01:36.260
and in this case is
three by three by three.

31
00:01:36.560 --> 00:01:39.690
The way you do this
is you would take

32
00:01:39.690 --> 00:01:42.445
this filter which
I'm going to draw as

33
00:01:42.445 --> 00:01:46.119
a three-dimensional yellow block

34
00:01:46.119 --> 00:01:48.685
and put the yellow
filter over there.

35
00:01:48.685 --> 00:01:53.065
There are 27 multiplications
you have to do,

36
00:01:53.065 --> 00:01:56.559
sum it up, and then that
gives you this value.

37
00:01:56.559 --> 00:01:58.740
Then shift a filter over,

38
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.105
multiply the 27 pairs of numbers,

39
00:02:01.105 --> 00:02:03.865
add that up that gives
you this number,

40
00:02:03.865 --> 00:02:06.415
and you keep going to get this,

41
00:02:06.415 --> 00:02:10.839
to get this, and then this,

42
00:02:10.839 --> 00:02:14.810
and so on, until you have

43
00:02:14.810 --> 00:02:18.875
computed all four by
four output values.

44
00:02:18.875 --> 00:02:21.350
We didn't use padding
in this case,

45
00:02:21.350 --> 00:02:22.670
and we use a stride of one,

46
00:02:22.670 --> 00:02:25.270
which is why the
output size a n out by

47
00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:30.170
a n out is a bit smaller
than the input size.

48
00:02:30.170 --> 00:02:33.995
That is this four by four
instead of six by six.

49
00:02:33.995 --> 00:02:36.230
Rather than just having one of

50
00:02:36.230 --> 00:02:38.255
these three by three
by three filters,

51
00:02:38.255 --> 00:02:44.025
you may have some
n_c prime filters

52
00:02:44.025 --> 00:02:45.770
and if you have five of them,

53
00:02:45.770 --> 00:02:50.375
then the output will be
four by four by five,

54
00:02:50.375 --> 00:02:55.740
or a n out by a n out by n_c prime.

55
00:02:55.740 --> 00:02:58.280
Let's figure out what is
the computational cost

56
00:02:58.280 --> 00:02:59.735
of what we just did.

57
00:02:59.735 --> 00:03:02.450
It turns out the total
number of computations

58
00:03:02.450 --> 00:03:05.600
needed to compute
this output is given

59
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:09.220
by the number of filter
parameters which is

60
00:03:09.220 --> 00:03:13.570
three by three by
three in this case,

61
00:03:13.570 --> 00:03:17.254
multiplied by the number
of filter positions.

62
00:03:17.254 --> 00:03:19.190
That is a number
of places where we

63
00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:21.760
place this big yellow block,

64
00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.405
which is four by four,

65
00:03:24.405 --> 00:03:27.815
and then multiplied by
the number of filters,

66
00:03:27.815 --> 00:03:29.880
which is five in this case.

67
00:03:29.880 --> 00:03:32.900
You can check for
yourself that this is

68
00:03:32.900 --> 00:03:36.080
the total number of
multiplications we need to do,

69
00:03:36.080 --> 00:03:37.220
because at each of

70
00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:39.395
the locations we plopped
down the filter,

71
00:03:39.395 --> 00:03:41.645
we need to do this
many multiplications,

72
00:03:41.645 --> 00:03:44.090
and then we have
this many filters.

73
00:03:44.090 --> 00:03:46.430
If you multiply
these numbers out,

74
00:03:46.430 --> 00:03:52.200
this turns out to be
2,160. We'll come back.

75
00:03:52.200 --> 00:03:54.520
You see this number again
later in this video,

76
00:03:54.520 --> 00:03:55.715
when we come up with

77
00:03:55.715 --> 00:03:58.045
the depthwise
separable convolution

78
00:03:58.045 --> 00:04:00.410
that will be able
to take as input

79
00:04:00.410 --> 00:04:05.330
a six by six by three image
and outputs four by four by

80
00:04:05.330 --> 00:04:07.790
five set of activations that were

81
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:10.855
fewer computations than 2,160.

82
00:04:10.855 --> 00:04:12.420
Let us see how the depthwise

83
00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:14.310
separable convolution does that.

84
00:04:14.310 --> 00:04:17.675
In contrast to the normal
convolution which you just saw,

85
00:04:17.675 --> 00:04:21.605
the depthwise separable
convolution has two steps.

86
00:04:21.605 --> 00:04:26.480
You're going to first use
a depthwise convolution,

87
00:04:26.480 --> 00:04:29.560
followed by a
pointwise convolution.

88
00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:32.960
It is these two steps
which together make

89
00:04:32.960 --> 00:04:36.480
up this depthwise
separable convolution.

90
00:04:36.480 --> 00:04:39.270
Let's see how each of
these two steps work.

91
00:04:39.270 --> 00:04:41.915
In particular, let's flesh out

92
00:04:41.915 --> 00:04:45.155
the details of how the
depthwise convolution,

93
00:04:45.155 --> 00:04:47.080
the first of these
two steps works.

94
00:04:47.080 --> 00:04:52.250
As before we have an input
that is six by six by three,

95
00:04:52.250 --> 00:04:57.309
so n by n by n_c, three channels.

96
00:04:57.309 --> 00:04:59.360
The filter and depthwise

97
00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.050
convolution is
going to be f by f,

98
00:05:03.050 --> 00:05:05.120
not f by f by n_c,

99
00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.780
but just f by f. The number of

100
00:05:08.780 --> 00:05:13.725
filters is going to be n_c,

101
00:05:13.725 --> 00:05:16.095
which in this case is three.

102
00:05:16.095 --> 00:05:19.060
The way that you will
compute the four by four

103
00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:22.030
by three output is that you

104
00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.190
apply one of each of

105
00:05:24.190 --> 00:05:29.530
these filters to one of each
of these input channels.

106
00:05:29.530 --> 00:05:31.720
Let's step through this.

107
00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:34.000
Let's focus on the first of

108
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.145
the three filters,
just the red one.

109
00:05:36.145 --> 00:05:39.805
Then take the red filter
and position it there,

110
00:05:39.805 --> 00:05:42.175
carry out the nine
multiplications.

111
00:05:42.175 --> 00:05:44.455
Notice this, on nine
numbers means multiply,

112
00:05:44.455 --> 00:05:47.440
not 27, but nine,
and add them up.

113
00:05:47.440 --> 00:05:49.105
That will give you this value.

114
00:05:49.105 --> 00:05:50.560
Then shift it over,

115
00:05:50.560 --> 00:05:53.920
multiply the nine corresponding
pairs of numbers,

116
00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:57.520
that gives you this
value over here,

117
00:05:57.520 --> 00:06:00.370
gives you that, gives you that,

118
00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:08.680
and so on, until you get to
the last of these 16 values.

119
00:06:08.680 --> 00:06:12.024
Next, we go to the
second channel,

120
00:06:12.024 --> 00:06:14.455
and let's look at
the green filter.

121
00:06:14.455 --> 00:06:16.915
The green filter,
you position there,

122
00:06:16.915 --> 00:06:19.960
carry out the nine multiplications
to compute this value,

123
00:06:19.960 --> 00:06:22.495
shift it over by one
to compute this value,

124
00:06:22.495 --> 00:06:25.360
shift over by one, and so on.

125
00:06:25.360 --> 00:06:28.900
You do that 16 times until
you've computed all of

126
00:06:28.900 --> 00:06:32.395
these values in the second
channel of the outputs.

127
00:06:32.395 --> 00:06:35.230
Finally, you do this
for the third channel,

128
00:06:35.230 --> 00:06:36.430
there's a blue filter,

129
00:06:36.430 --> 00:06:40.525
position it there to compute
this value, shift it over,

130
00:06:40.525 --> 00:06:44.845
shift it over, shift
it over, and so on,

131
00:06:44.845 --> 00:06:49.045
until you've computed
all 16 outputs

132
00:06:49.045 --> 00:06:52.300
in that third channel as well.

133
00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:55.750
The size of the output
after this step will

134
00:06:55.750 --> 00:06:59.020
be n out by n out,

135
00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:04.105
four-by-four, by nc, where
nc is the same as this nc;

136
00:07:04.105 --> 00:07:06.940
the number of channels
in your original input.

137
00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:09.715
Let's look at the
computational cost

138
00:07:09.715 --> 00:07:11.290
of what we've just done.

139
00:07:11.290 --> 00:07:13.900
Because to generate each
of these output values,

140
00:07:13.900 --> 00:07:16.360
each of these 4 by 4
by 3 output values,

141
00:07:16.360 --> 00:07:19.435
it required nine multiplications.

142
00:07:19.435 --> 00:07:22.465
The total computational
cost is 3 by 3

143
00:07:22.465 --> 00:07:26.844
times the number of
filter positions,

144
00:07:26.844 --> 00:07:29.890
that is, the number
of positions each of

145
00:07:29.890 --> 00:07:33.430
these filters was placed on
top of the image on the left.

146
00:07:33.430 --> 00:07:36.070
There was 4 by 4,
and then finally,

147
00:07:36.070 --> 00:07:39.175
times the number of
filters, which is 3.

148
00:07:39.175 --> 00:07:41.620
Another way to look
at this is that you

149
00:07:41.620 --> 00:07:45.685
have 4 by 4 by 3 outputs,

150
00:07:45.685 --> 00:07:47.710
and for each of those outputs,

151
00:07:47.710 --> 00:07:51.475
you needed to carry out
nine multiplications.

152
00:07:51.475 --> 00:07:54.640
The total computational
cost is 3 times

153
00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:57.715
3 times 4 times 4 times 3.

154
00:07:57.715 --> 00:07:59.440
If you multiply
all these numbers,

155
00:07:59.440 --> 00:08:02.860
it turns out to be 432.

156
00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:04.900
But we're not yet done.

157
00:08:04.900 --> 00:08:07.915
This is a depth-wise
convolution part

158
00:08:07.915 --> 00:08:10.705
of the depth wise
separable convolution.

159
00:08:10.705 --> 00:08:12.490
There's one more step, which is,

160
00:08:12.490 --> 00:08:17.290
we need to take this 4 by
4 by 3 intermediate value

161
00:08:17.290 --> 00:08:20.305
and carry out one more step.

162
00:08:20.305 --> 00:08:23.710
The remaining step
is to take this 4 by

163
00:08:23.710 --> 00:08:28.630
4 by 3 set of values,

164
00:08:28.630 --> 00:08:30.550
or n out by n out by nc set of

165
00:08:30.550 --> 00:08:33.700
values and apply a
pointwise convolution in

166
00:08:33.700 --> 00:08:36.370
order to get the output
we want which will

167
00:08:36.370 --> 00:08:39.805
be 4 by 4 by 5.

168
00:08:39.805 --> 00:08:42.760
Let's see how the pointwise
convolution works.

169
00:08:42.760 --> 00:08:45.340
Here's the pointwise convolution.

170
00:08:45.340 --> 00:08:49.360
We are going to take the
intermediate set of values,

171
00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:55.180
which is n out by n out by nc,

172
00:08:55.180 --> 00:09:02.320
and convolve it with a
filter that is 1 by 1 by nc,

173
00:09:02.320 --> 00:09:04.510
1 by 1 by 3 in this case.

174
00:09:04.510 --> 00:09:07.405
Well, you take this pink filter,

175
00:09:07.405 --> 00:09:10.675
this pink 1 by 1 by 3 block,

176
00:09:10.675 --> 00:09:13.495
and apply it to upper
left-most position,

177
00:09:13.495 --> 00:09:16.270
carry out the three
multiplications,

178
00:09:16.270 --> 00:09:19.675
add them up, and that
gives you this value,

179
00:09:19.675 --> 00:09:21.400
shift it over by one,

180
00:09:21.400 --> 00:09:24.070
multiply the three pairs
of numbers, add them up,

181
00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:27.865
that gives you that
value, and so on.

182
00:09:27.865 --> 00:09:31.300
You keep going
until you've filled

183
00:09:31.300 --> 00:09:34.660
out all 16 values of this output.

184
00:09:34.660 --> 00:09:38.020
Now, we've done this
with just one filter in

185
00:09:38.020 --> 00:09:41.965
order to get another
4 by 4 output,

186
00:09:41.965 --> 00:09:44.605
by the 4 by 4 by 5
dimensional outputs,

187
00:09:44.605 --> 00:09:49.840
you would actually do this
with nc prime filters,

188
00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:52.525
in this case nc
prime will set to 5.

189
00:09:52.525 --> 00:09:55.840
So a five of these 1
by 1 by 3 filters,

190
00:09:55.840 --> 00:10:01.195
you end up with a 4 by 4
by 5 output as follows,

191
00:10:01.195 --> 00:10:04.165
to give you an n out by n

192
00:10:04.165 --> 00:10:08.710
out by nc prime
dimensional output.

193
00:10:08.710 --> 00:10:11.500
The pointwise convolution gives

194
00:10:11.500 --> 00:10:14.785
you the 4 by 4 by 5 output.

195
00:10:14.785 --> 00:10:17.200
Let's figure out the
computational cost

196
00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:18.370
of what we just did.

197
00:10:18.370 --> 00:10:20.260
For every one of these four

198
00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:22.345
by four by five output values,

199
00:10:22.345 --> 00:10:27.040
we had to apply this pink
filter to part of the input.

200
00:10:27.040 --> 00:10:33.150
That cost three operations
or 1 by 1 by 1,

201
00:10:33.150 --> 00:10:35.405
which is number of
filter parameters.

202
00:10:35.405 --> 00:10:38.080
The filtered had to be placed in

203
00:10:38.080 --> 00:10:41.470
4 by 4 different positions,

204
00:10:41.470 --> 00:10:44.125
and we had five filters.

205
00:10:44.125 --> 00:10:47.290
To tell the cost of what
we just did here is,

206
00:10:47.290 --> 00:10:50.740
1 times 1 times 3 times
4 times 4 times 5,

207
00:10:50.740 --> 00:10:54.760
which is 240 multiplications.

208
00:10:54.760 --> 00:10:56.830
In the example we just walked

209
00:10:56.830 --> 00:10:58.990
through, the normal convolution,

210
00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:01.825
took as input a six by
six by three input,

211
00:11:01.825 --> 00:11:05.995
and wound up with a four
by four by five outwards.

212
00:11:05.995 --> 00:11:09.490
Same for the depthwise
separable convolution,

213
00:11:09.490 --> 00:11:11.350
except we did it in two steps

214
00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.600
with a depthwise convolution,

215
00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:16.825
followed by a
pointwise convolution.

216
00:11:16.825 --> 00:11:20.395
Now, what were the
computational costs

217
00:11:20.395 --> 00:11:22.495
of all of these operations?

218
00:11:22.495 --> 00:11:25.075
In the case of the
normal convolution,

219
00:11:25.075 --> 00:11:30.955
we needed 2160 multiplications
to compute the output.

220
00:11:30.955 --> 00:11:33.985
For the depthwise
separable convolution,

221
00:11:33.985 --> 00:11:37.630
there was first,
the depthwise step,

222
00:11:37.630 --> 00:11:40.105
where we had from
earlier in the video,

223
00:11:40.105 --> 00:11:45.445
432 multiplications and
then the pointwise step,

224
00:11:45.445 --> 00:11:49.750
where we had 240 multiplications,

225
00:11:49.750 --> 00:11:51.640
and so adding these up,

226
00:11:51.640 --> 00:11:57.010
we wind up with 672
multiplications.

227
00:11:57.010 --> 00:12:00.069
If we look the ratio
between these two numbers,

228
00:12:00.069 --> 00:12:07.210
672 over 2160, this turns
out to be about 0.31.

229
00:12:07.210 --> 00:12:10.689
In this example, the depthwise
separable convolution

230
00:12:10.689 --> 00:12:12.655
was about 31 percent

231
00:12:12.655 --> 00:12:14.860
as computationally expensive as

232
00:12:14.860 --> 00:12:18.595
the normal convolution,
roughly [inaudible] savings.

233
00:12:18.595 --> 00:12:22.195
The authors of the
MobileNets paper

234
00:12:22.195 --> 00:12:24.325
showed that in general,

235
00:12:24.325 --> 00:12:27.190
the ratio of the cost of

236
00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:30.039
the depthwise
separable convolution

237
00:12:30.039 --> 00:12:32.065
compared to the
normal convolution,

238
00:12:32.065 --> 00:12:35.784
that turns out to be
equal to one over

239
00:12:35.784 --> 00:12:41.425
n_c prime plus one over f
squared in the general case.

240
00:12:41.425 --> 00:12:44.530
In our case, this was
one over five plus

241
00:12:44.530 --> 00:12:48.745
one over three squared or one
over nine, which is 0.31.

242
00:12:48.745 --> 00:12:51.985
In a more typical
neural network example,

243
00:12:51.985 --> 00:12:54.400
n_c prime will be much bigger.

244
00:12:54.400 --> 00:12:59.305
so It may be, say, one over 512.

245
00:12:59.305 --> 00:13:02.200
If you have 512 channels in

246
00:13:02.200 --> 00:13:05.980
your output plus one
over three squared,

247
00:13:05.980 --> 00:13:10.315
this would be a fairly typical
parameters or new network.

248
00:13:10.315 --> 00:13:13.945
This is really small
and this is one-ninth.

249
00:13:13.945 --> 00:13:15.670
So very roughly,

250
00:13:15.670 --> 00:13:17.890
the depthwise
separable convolution

251
00:13:17.890 --> 00:13:20.125
may be about one-ninth,

252
00:13:20.125 --> 00:13:22.300
rounding up roughly 10

253
00:13:22.300 --> 00:13:27.520
times cheaper in
computational costs.

254
00:13:27.520 --> 00:13:30.339
That's why the depthwise
